// cntrdct external-source audit corpus manifest. One JSON object per non-blank line.
// Lines starting with `//` are skipped by the loader. See README.md for schema and source list.
//
// Phase B batch 1 (2026-05-11): six expected findings across two detectors
// (unreachable-after-terminator x5, arg-swap x1) and two sources
// (rustc-lint-testset, github-commit).
//
// Phase B batch 2 (2026-05-12): six additional expected findings, all
// `unreachable-after-terminator` on `rustc-lint-testset`, probing
// control-flow shapes the detector misses by construction
// (return-as-expression-tail, return-as-call-argument, divergent-loop
// terminator). Deepens the recall ceiling on the existing detector
// rather than broadening detector coverage; further batches still owe
// the remaining four detectors and the semgrep / codeql / clippy /
// paper-appendix source kinds. See `docs/spec/recall-audit-v0.md` and
// the README "Latest audit run" for the refreshed figures.
//
// Phase B batch 3 (2026-05-12): broadens the corpus to a third
// detector, `comment-code`, via the textbook Pattern C bug (the
// `/// Deprecated` prose / `#[deprecated]` attribute mismatch Tan
// SOSP 2007 §3.2 calls "bad comment"). Source: Apache-2.0
// `sidan-lab/whisky-archive` `packages/whisky-common/src/data/
// primitives/constructors.rs` pinned at commit `99243766`. The
// `con_str` / `con_str0` / `con_str1` / `con_str2` family documents
// itself as deprecated in prose but ships without the runtime
// `#[deprecated]` attribute that Rust's deprecation lints honour,
// so downstream consumers get no compiler warning. Four expected
// TP entries; clone-drift was investigated first per the original
// batch 3 plan but punted to a later batch because the published
// peer-reviewed clone-drift bug catalogues (Bettenburg MSR 2009,
// Krinke ICSM 2007) target C/Java rather than Rust/Python, and the
// Assi TOSEM 2025 deep-learning-framework genealogies expose size-2
// clone pairs that fall under cntrdct's `MIN_GROUP_SIZE = 3` floor
// by construction.
//
// All rustc-testset files strip the upstream
// `#![deny(unreachable_code)]` attribute because cntrdct's
// SUPPRESSION_TOKEN scan otherwise treats it as an in-source
// suppression; the per-line `//~ ERROR ...` annotations are preserved
// verbatim as documentation.
//
// Phase B batch 4 (2026-05-12): three additional expected entries on
// `arg-swap`, all sourced from PyPIBugs (Allamanis NeurIPS 2021) — the
// first `paper-appendix` source kind in the corpus. PyPIBugs ships
// 311 labelled ArgSwap bugs under MIT (zipped JSONL at Microsoft
// Download Center). Three entries selected from the
// `idxs=0<->1` partition with verified-permissive upstream
// repositories: c137digital/unv_app (MIT, cross-file import call),
// mwouts/nbrmd (MIT, cross-file import call in a test), and
// markokr/rarfile (ISC, in-class method call). All three are FN
// against cntrdct's narrow Rice-2017 arg-swap detector by
// docs/spec/arg-swap-v0.md F3 / F4: F4 only resolves same-file
// definitions (cross-file imports yield no callee match), and F3
// drops qualified-path / method-call call sites entirely. The batch
// adds honest selection-bias signal to the audit: PyPIBugs labels
// arg-swap bugs that cntrdct cannot catch by detector design, so
// arg-swap's audit recall_upper_bound is bounded by the strict
// same-file + bare-identifier resolution, not by the bug class's
// real prevalence. pr-miner was the originally chosen batch-4
// detector but punted to a later batch on the structural blocker
// that pr-miner's extractor walks only top-level `fn` / `def`
// (src/detectors/pr_miner/extract_{rust,python}.rs), which collides
// with modern Rust RAII and Python `with` idioms — paired-API
// patterns survive almost exclusively in class methods that the
// extractor drops.
